    "turn_idx": 1,
    "turn_text": "Hello, I'm Congressman Tim Burchin. Welcome back to another episode of Tennessee Talks. Today we're going to do a little things a little different and I'm going to answer some questions that were sent in by some of y'all. Some were asked by some of the followers on Twitter and we'll have to clean up some of those obviously. And while the others were submitted online in a survey that I posted, The first question we have is Greg Stanley from Merville, Tennessee asks, what is the likelihood of impeachment moving forward for Joe Biden to multiple other high-level members of his administration, given the high level of evidence supporting the dereliction of duty and abusive power within the offices held? I'd say it's very, very likely that that will occur. But I think what you got to remember is, you know, we can hang as many impeachments on the president, but it doesn't mean he's out of office. And what we get about, we're not probably wanting to get him a Senate. I think the thing that's most discouraging about that is the Justice Department just won't do anything. You're going to do a deadgum thing. And so that's where we've got a real problem. But I just warn people of that. And I think too that We need to make sure we still continue our focus on our budget situation and our reckless spending that just continues to happen. And we'll happen in the future. At Gypsy Marine 42 on Twitter asks, thank you for continuing the fight, the good fight on the UFO issue at acclimation point. Could you give us an update on where everything stands with the select committee? Well, the select committee was addressed in a letter Matt Gaetz pinned and myself and I represent Luna and Jared Moskowitz, I believe out of Florida, all three of them are out of Florida, of course, at where we've asked for a select committee. And once a select committee would have subpoena power. and it would provide the folks, the whistleblowers, some protection. And again, it keeps us out of the skiff, because anything we say in the skiff is sacred, and therefore it won't be released to public. And everybody said, get in the skiff with them, get in the skiff with them. I don't need to get in the skiff with them. I already, you know, I've seen and heard it up. I would, um, I would like for us to have this select committee. And again, Speaker McCarthy has to be the one to okay it. And then he would pass it on to Chairman Comer. And then the committee would be formed that way. Jerry Hardwick from Lewisville, Tennessee. If you're from out of town, you say Louisville, Tennessee. But if you're from here, it's Lewisville, Tennessee asks, Over the past many years, time and again, Congress talks about Social Security running short of money. However, I have never heard once of any concerns about welfare running out of money. Why is welfare more important than Social Security? I don't know, that's more important. I think it's just the priorities, and they always will show you the worst case scenario to get the funding, just like when they stop funding of something that the folks in power, they'll show you the worst. What do we always do? We always cut off funding to our seniors and our veterans when it gets tough and we ought to be looking at just a myriad of other ridiculous spending that we do. And yet that seems to be where the focus is and because that's where you get most of your airplane clickbait as it is now. Rodney Grubb from Knoxville, Tennessee asked, what can be done to expedite term limits and separately deal with the existing decrepit old incapacitated elected representatives? Couldn't get that worn out. For the record, I am 67 and retired and people who only seem to believe in term limits for other people's representatives. And I agree with you. I've supported every term limits bill that's out there. You know, I got a feeling we're getting ready to pass something in the House in Newt Gingrich's contract with America out of all the things that that was the one thing he couldn't pass or he couldn't get it out of committee. And so I think we're close to it as a constitutional amendment. But again, I don't think it's going to be brought up in the Senate and we'll probably pass something And it will, I'm going to be pretty good, but I'm pretty sure that it will die and untimely death in the Senate. And we will never, it will never see the line today. And, you know, like in Knoxville, we just had a city race, I think less than 15% of the people voted. So we've, it's clearly an issue that, that, It'll pull through the roof, but nobody holds anybody accountable on Election Day to do it. So eventually, I hope to get something to the table that I'm going to vote on. But again, don't keep your fingers crossed. And if we do pass something in the house, it probably won't even be brought up in the Senate. At LG STR 55, Leakster 55 on Twitter asks, Are they going to be any UAP field hearings? And UAP is another number of the federal government gate to UFOs. And I suspect there could be, but field hearings, anybody can hold a field hearing and we can call people in and do those things. But do we get anything from it and we can't provide any whistleblower protection and I'm afraid our whistleblower protection as was shown in this last hearings that, you know, they leak that the guy at one of our top witnesses had. experience some some trauma in his life and and to me that just blew the doors off of any whistleblower protection right there and that's exactly why we need a select committee so we can call these people in and without fear of retribution and I'm afraid that that we're gonna this thing is gonna they're gonna shut us down on this issue for now. And I'm not, I have a lot of theories why, but probably don't have enough time on this podcast to go into it. But we were definitely over the target because there was, we, I mean, from staff members in our own party to other congressmen to, you know, the alphabet agencies, the Pentagon, everybody put an obstruction up even when we want to have a press conference, they tried to curtail that and put us in a small room that was basically under construction. Let's see, Steve, handle like us from Powell to the CS. What's your stand on the climate change issue? Well, I believe climate change is real. I do believe that, and I believe we should be better stewards of our land. I'm an organic gardener and farmer really. I don't grow much other than grass, We don't use any chemicals that don't occur naturally, say we don't use chemicals, we use horse manure, and that's obviously a chemical. And so I'm very much in tune to that, and I can remember as a kid, driving with my parents going to Church Street, United Methodist, and on VW Bug, and the windows would be down, we'd pass Robert Shaw. And literally, if they were making plexiglassier eyes with water and your nose would run, it was that. the smell was that future and it was just that rough and you know we've we're doing a long you'd ask people today it's the worst it's ever been the environment no it's not it's it's probably the best it's been in a hundred years actually because we've got a lot of restraints but we seem to be the only country that follows that and every time we we tie our hands on one of these treaties uh india rush china you name it They don't follow it. And we're the only ones that do. And it just further puts us behind the eight ball. We really want to do something. I think we ought to follow what God's plan is. And we need to plant more dead gum trees. It's proven that if we planted a trillion trees, which is a huge number, but we're $32 trillion in debt. So if you can extrapolate that a little bit out across the entire globe, though, if we did that, we could set carbon back. carbon to restrain it more than the system is basically what it does. And we can do a lot for the environment that way, but there's not a lot of movement to that. As a matter of fact, I've seen literally videos within just the last month of people saying that some areas were playing too many trees. Well, what they're saying is we're We do what's called a mono culture. If you plant just white ponds, you're going to develop a problem. You need to plant diverse different kinds of trees the way it is in nature. So you don't develop those type of problems. And I think that can go a long ways towards fixing the environment, provide shade in the summer. And it's just a natural habitat for animals and birds and things. So I think it's a That would be a great, I'm a big fan of that and I've read a lot of research on it and I think it could be done within our entire planet. If we had some agreements that the UN would quit worrying about how many hot knobs people eat, literally to solving some of the problems that we've so well funded them to solve that maybe they would, we could see some improvement. Jan Hedrick from Maribel, Tennessee asked, what is the plan for our mentally ill population? The issues of gun control and crimes are directly linked to mental illness. Why don't we treat the cause instead of worrying about gun control? That's a good question. I suspect a lot of it has to do with the stigma attached to mentally ill folks. I deal with that in our office with people just today. I took a call from a friend. who is experiencing some mental health issues and has in the past, and the stigma attached to it is still pretty, pretty great. You know, you go, you cut your hand, you don't think anything, run them out over to UT Hospital Mercy's room and get it getting stitches, but if something's If you have a thought or something's going on inside your head, it maybe isn't right. You're depressed. There's an incredible stigma attached to that. And there really shouldn't because it's so it can be treated. A lot of our mental illness can be treated. And so I think it's just not an end to mental health. It's not a real sexy issue. It doesn't grab headlines, but we whereas gun violence does with 48,000. people died I think 2022 maybe a gun death about half of those you remove half for suicide but and you would think they would use some other method if they didn't have a gun I don't know but that in context over 70,000 people in 2021 died of fentanyl and it's just exploding and you can't get anybody in the White House or leadership to to even bring it up. because it's flowing in over our southern border. And they'd have to admit we have a problem. A lot of politics involved. And the constitutionality of the Second Amendment is pretty clear to me. So where do you draw that line? That's going to be the tough thing. But I think I do know that in Tennessee, mental health affects one in five Tennesseans. If it doesn't affect you, probably a family member or maybe multiple family members, I don't know. So it's a major problem. And we should, we need to address it more. And we always say that we need to put more funding, but I think more understanding of it could go a long ways and in the actual treatment of what actually works and what doesn't. I think there's a lot of duplication and we've seen in the past where prescription drugs are given out to people that maybe they need. maybe more counseling or maybe they need more prescription drugs, but and also, um, they'll issue maybe a month's worth to somebody. And then they feel like they're okay. So like when you get a call, well, I'm not the sniffles anymore. I'm great. And then they get back off of it and they have another episode, which is even greater. And sometimes it can take over 30 days for the, for the chemical effect to take over their system and get their, get their brain aligned correctly. And so there's a lot of, there's a lot of different things involved with that. So I'm, and two, when we deregulated the mental health industry and just put all those folks out on the street, that was, it's never a one size fits all surely some of them probably didn't need to be institutionalized. But at that time, you go under any, any bridge and Knoxville today and you can You can just about find somebody and we think probably around half those folks are mentally ill. So it's a real problem and we're not addressing it right now. Just throwing more money at the problem is not going to address it. And that's not an easy answer, Jan. And some people are going to have to just have to deal with the real facts and they won't do that. The only time they ever deal with is when we have a calamity and The person was mentally ill and they go back and look at it. It seems like we could do a lot more preventive than we do. But that, again, it doesn't sell. It's not click right. It doesn't gin up the support. So we tend to demonize things and then look for a cure. We should have cured it on the front end. But anyway, I want to thank everybody for the questions. And if y'all just keep them coming, we'll answer them. I love to answer questions, especially if I don't. I'll try to get back to you. But anyway, I want to thank everybody for the great questions. And I'm sorry we couldn't answer them all in the limited time frame. And I'm Congressman Tim Burch and I want to thank everybody to listen to another episode of Tennessee Talks. And thank y'all for sitting here.",
